Build node-pty on FreeBSD.

Upstream node-pty 1.2.0-beta.15 defines pty_close_inherited_fds() (and its
SetCloseOnExec() helper) inside `#if defined(__linux__)`, but calls it
unconditionally in the post-fork child, right before execvp(). On FreeBSD that
is a hard compile error:

  src/unix/pty.cc:477:7: error: use of undeclared identifier
      'pty_close_inherited_fds'

The fix is to compile the same helper on FreeBSD. The Linux implementation is
already portable apart from its close_range() fast path, and FreeBSD has had
close_range(2) with CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC since 13.0 — declared in
<unistd.h>:506, flag in <sys/unistd.h>:217 on this 15.1 box — so it is used
directly as a libc call rather than through syscall(SYS_close_range, ...),
which is the Linux-specific spelling. The fcntl() loop stays as the fallback
for both platforms, unchanged.

Semantics are deliberately identical to Linux: descriptors are marked
FD_CLOEXEC, not closed. closefrom(3) would close them outright — a different
behaviour, and not what the caller expects.

Verified on the dev VM (185, FreeBSD 15.1-RC2, node 24.14.1, node-gyp 12.4.0):
node-pty 1.2.0-beta.15 compiles clean with this patch and works at runtime --
spawning /bin/sh through forkpty(3) returns its output and exit code 0.

--- a/src/unix/pty.cc	2026-08-26 13:40:44.514396000 +0000
+++ b/src/unix/pty.cc	2026-08-26 13:40:44.527467000 +0000
@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@
   int exit_code = 0, signal_code = 0;
 };
 
-#if defined(__linux__)
+#if defined(__linux__) || defined(__FreeBSD__)
 
 static int
 SetCloseOnExec(int fd) {
@@ -130,8 +130,15 @@
  */
 static void
 pty_close_inherited_fds() {
+  // FreeBSD 13+ exposes close_range(2) as a plain libc function; Linux
+  // reaches the same syscall through syscall(SYS_close_range, ...).
+  #if defined(__FreeBSD__) && defined(CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC)
+  if (close_range(3, ~0U, CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC) == 0) {
+    return;
+  }
+  #endif
   // Try close_range() first (Linux 5.9+, glibc 2.34+)
-  #if defined(SYS_close_range) && defined(CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC)
+  #if defined(__linux__) && defined(SYS_close_range) && defined(CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC)
   if (syscall(SYS_close_range, 3, ~0U, CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC) == 0) {
     return;
   }
